From 52811c9fbb6ba844427dd79f74af0bed1bab0eba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:38:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard tests: a closed group's redirection
 ends at the next operator

Post-merge mutant check of PR #2442 (card t_01a1d66730bdf51a) found that
dropping `self.closing = None` in `_LineWalk.operator`
(cron/lifecycle_guard_groups.py) survived all 15 lifecycle-guard test files.
Under it, `closing` from a subshell `)` survives an empty segment before the
next operator, so the next command's `< r.sh` is credited to the subshell and
its `bash` is treated as reading the file: a false refusal.

Add four allowed cases to test_group_redirections_and_data_stay_allowed:
  (bash); grep x < r.sh
  (bash) && cat < r.sh
  (bash) | grep x < r.sh
  { bash; }; grep x < r.sh

Test-only; production is correct.

Mutant proof (sdlc-review scripts/mutants.py, predictions written first):
  baseline: 46 passed
  M4 (operator reset dropped): killed, 3 failed (the three subshell cases)
  M5 (flush reset dropped) alone: survives, equivalent (operator resets after
    every flush)
  M4+M5: killed, 4 failed (adds `{ bash; }; grep x < r.sh`)
scripts/run_tests.sh on the file: 46 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped.
---
 tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_group_redirections.py | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_group_redirections.py b/tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_group_redirections.py
index d87d8c58a4..b7e808feec 100644
--- a/tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_group_redirections.py
+++ b/tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_group_redirections.py
@@ -117,6 +117,11 @@ def test_group_and_glued_stdin_feeds_are_scanned(files, template):
         "{{ bash {clean_sh}; {{ true; }} }} < {sh}",
         "{{ {{ true; }} }} < {sh}; bash < {clean_sh}",
         "{{ echo }}; }} < {sh}",
+        # A closed group's redirection ends at the next operator: the next command's `<` is its own.
+        "(bash); grep x < {sh}",
+        "(bash) && cat < {sh}",
+        "(bash) | grep x < {sh}",
+        "{{ bash; }}; grep x < {sh}",
         # Data: the redirection names the file, nothing runs it.
         "echo '{{ bash; }} < {sh}'",
         "grep -c x < {sh}",
-- 
